p**.\n\nOverall, the impossibility part of the solution contains gaps in reasoning, but none of the gaps invalidate the final conclusion because the conclusions happen to be correct.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Constructions  \n\n- **\\(k=0\\):** Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n  **Verification:** Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\). All lines are non‑sunny. Construction is correct.\n\n- **\\(k=1\\):** Use vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n  \\[\n  L:\\; y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}(x-n).\n  \\]  \n  **Verification:** The slope \\(\\frac{1}{1-n}=-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\); thus \\(L\\) is sunny. It passes through \\((n,1)\\); all other points have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the appropriate vertical line. Construction is correct.\n\n- **\\(k=3\\):** Define the three sunny lines (5) and supplement with vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  **Verification:** Direct substitution shows each line passes through the two claimed lattice points; together they cover all points of the three rightmost columns. The vertical lines cover the remaining columns. The slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\) are all sunny. The total number of lines is \\((n-3)+3=n\\). Construction is correct for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nNo errors are present in the construction part.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Completion of the proof  \n\n- The solution correctly states that the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) and that the constructions verify each case.  \n- The only deficiencies are the gaps identified in Section 3; they do not affect the correctness of the final answer.\n\n---"
