1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:\n> * \\(p=q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(1\\));\n> * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(-2\\));\n> * \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\)\u2003(slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap (factual mis\u2011statement).*  \nFor the second pair \\((p,q)=(-2,1)\\) we have \\(|p+q|=|-1|=1\\), not \\(2\\).  Likewise for \\((p,q)=(-1,2)\\) we have \\(|p+q|=|1|=1\\).  Only the first pair satisfies \\(|p+q|=2\\).  Thus the claim that \u201c\\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities\u201d is factually incorrect.  The three listed lines are indeed sunny and later shown to cover the required points for \\(k=3\\), but the classification is wrong.\n\n> \u201cFor \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point\u2011sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.*  \nThe statement \u201cNo other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points\u201d is not proved.  For larger odd \\(k\\) there exist sunny lines with \\(|p+q|=1\\) and \\(q=1\\) (e.g. \\(p=-2,q=1\\)) that can attain the bound for certain values of \\(k\\) (e.g. \\(k=7\\) the line \\(y=-2x+9\\) contains \\(4\\) points).  Hence the argument that the only possible lines are the three listed is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Consequence for the size of \\(k\\)\n\n> \u201cThus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only**\n> the three lines (10).  Hence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\); i.e. \\(k=1\\) or\n> \\(k=3\\).\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.*  \nThe deduction \u201cwe must use only the three lines (10)\u201d rests on the previous (incorrect) claim that no other sunny line can achieve the bound.  As shown above,